 ## Working approach (checkpoint-first, then iterate to fidelity)
 Soft budget of about 15 minutes — enough for several refinement passes. Use it; do not stop at the first valid solid.

-1. **Build a dimension table (read the drawing once).** Write out, as text: the overall envelope; every diameter and bolt circle; the full through-thickness profile from the section view (each thickness step, hub/boss height, counterbore and pocket depth); the count and positions of holes/pockets; and fillet/chamfer sizes. A few targeted zoom-crops to read specific callouts are fine. Once the table is complete, stop re-reading the image for DIMENSIONS — but keep the isometric/section as a FORM reference (which way a curve arcs, where draft or taper runs, where a face is domed); do not flatten a clearly curved body into a constant-section extrusion just because the table is locked. Do NOT trace or extract the part's outline from the pixels (edge/contour detection, flood-fill, thresholding) — dimension and leader lines pollute it and it burns the budget; reconstruct the geometry parametrically from the printed dimensions instead. **Encode the table as named parameters, not inline numbers.** In your first execute(), bind every dimension from the table to a named top-level variable with a unit comment (`plate_thickness = 12.0  # mm`, `bolt_circle_dia = 80.0  # mm`, `hole_count = 6`), and build every feature by referencing those names — never re-type a raw dimension into a primitive, coordinate or Location. Derive dependent values by formula from the named ones (`hole_radius = clearance_dia / 2`) so a single transcription error cannot diverge two places. This changes only the code structure, never the geometry, but it eliminates arithmetic-slip errors and keeps every dimension checkable against your table in one place.
+1. **Build a dimension table (read the drawing once).** Write out, as text: the overall envelope; every diameter and bolt circle; the full through-thickness profile from the section view (each thickness step, hub/boss height, counterbore and pocket depth); the count and positions of holes/pockets; and fillet/chamfer sizes. **Before the first execute(), classify every stacked dimension as either an overall span or a component span and write one arithmetic chain per affected axis** (for example `overall_height = base_height + body_height + boss_height`, so `body_height = overall_height - base_height - boss_height`). Determine the span from the dimension extension-line endpoints: a value spanning the base to the boss top is already overall and must not have the boss height added again. Reject any interpretation whose computed final bbox differs from a printed overall dimension. A few targeted zoom-crops to read specific callouts are fine. Once the table is complete, stop re-reading the image for DIMENSIONS — but keep the isometric/section as a FORM reference (which way a curve arcs, where draft or taper runs, where a face is domed); do not flatten a clearly curved body into a constant-section extrusion just because the table is locked. Do NOT trace or extract the part's outline from the pixels (edge/contour detection, flood-fill, thresholding) — dimension and leader lines pollute it and it burns the budget; reconstruct the geometry parametrically from the printed dimensions instead. **Encode the table as named parameters, not inline numbers.** In your first execute(), bind every dimension from the table to a named top-level variable with a unit comment (`plate_thickness = 12.0  # mm`, `bolt_circle_dia = 80.0  # mm`, `hole_count = 6`), and build every feature by referencing those names — never re-type a raw dimension into a primitive, coordinate or Location. Derive dependent values by formula from the named ones (`hole_radius = clearance_dia / 2`) so a single transcription error cannot diverge two places. This changes only the code structure, never the geometry, but it eliminates arithmetic-slip errors and keeps every dimension checkable against your table in one place.

 2. **Build the side profile first, then the plan features.** The section/side view defines the Z-profile — model every thickness step, raised hub/boss, counterbore and pocket depth BEFORE cutting the hole pattern. Do NOT extrude the plan outline at one uniform thickness unless the section genuinely shows a flat plate of constant thickness: a correct plan view on a wrongly-flattened body scores poorly on BOTH surface distance and volume. The section also reveals WALL THICKNESS — covers, housings, brackets and castings are usually thin-walled shells or cored bodies, not solid blocks; model the shell/cored body as part of the primary construction (offset/shell the outer form, or subtract an inner offset) and hollow out cored/recessed regions before finishing, because a thin-walled part built as a solid block leaves far too much material and tanks the volume-overlap score. Then add the outer profile detail, central bore and hole pattern. After booleans, confirm with measure() (key dimensions), cross_sections(axis="Z") (through-thickness profile against your table) and find_bosses() (any hub/boss the drawing shows is present). **Before a boolean construction step you are not confident in** — fusing a boss/rib onto the base, cutting or trimming a feature back, anything where the obvious naive approach might leave a coincident face or remove more than intended — read the build123d://skill/modeling resource first. It is field-proven technique for exactly these failure modes (e.g. a boss fused on a coincident footprint silently failing to fuse, or a cut sized to a feature's rough extent removing adjacent material), not a generic hint; a guessed construction can pass validate() while still being the wrong shape or leaving avoidable topology problems that only show up later.

